<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:16:Real Mode">
<!--
    kuna #1288: in an x86 real-mode image, every near CALL/JMP/Jcc rel16 above
    linear 0x10000 branched into the first 64 KiB.

    Real-mode addresses are linear, CS*16 + IP, and a code segment need not
    start on a 64 KiB boundary. kuna's GH-7418 fix (tests/stages/
    gh7418-x86-call-rel16-mask.xml) masks a rel16 target to its low 16 bits,
    which is right for a 0x66-prefixed branch in 32-bit code but was also
    applied to 16-bit code: a DOS program loaded at segment 0x1000 lost every
    call target, so discovery found 12 of its functions instead of 330, and
    calls landed in data as phantom functions.

        1fff0  e8 00 70     CALL 0x26ff3      ; 0x1fff3 + 0x7000
        1fff3  c3           RET
        26ff3  b8 07 00     MOV  AX,0x7
        26ff6  c3           RET

    Stage: P1/P2 decode (x86 SLEIGH spec fix, no option). `rel16` now splits
    on the decode mode: with a 0x66 prefix (32-bit code) the GH-7418 mask
    stays; in real mode the target is the linear sum; in 16-bit protected
    mode the offset wraps inside its selector, as upstream computes it (see
    kuna-x86-16-protected-rel16.xml).

    Assert 1 is the decoded target, assert 2 forbids the masked 0x6ff3, and
    assert 3 is the call reaching the function that is there.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1fff0">
e80070c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x26ff3">
b80700c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1fff0" name="f"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x26ff3" name="g"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>disassemble 0x1fff0 0x1fff4</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #1: CALL rel16 at 0x1fff0 reaches the linear target 0x26ff3" min="1" max="1">^0x0001fff0: CALL +0x26ff3</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #2: the target is not masked into the first 64 KiB" min="0" max="0">0x6ff3</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #3: f calls g" min="1" max="1">^  g\(\);$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
